6146 opencode run --model opencode/space-bunny-free --dir /home/runner/work/rbops/rbops/redblue --agent build --title rbops-phase-084 # DO THIS, NOW. No orientation phase.  Your working directory is the project checkout. It is the ONLY place you may read or write. The RBOPS pipeline that invoked you lives elsewhere; do not inspect it, do not audit it, and do not spend a single tool call on it.  You have one job, described below. Work in this order and do not deviate:  1. Reproduce the finding. Smallest command that shows it. Then STOP and note it. 2. Write ONE failing test that demonstrates it. Run it. Watch it fail. 3. Make the smallest change that turns that test green. 4. Add the remaining edge-case tests required below. 5. Run the four gates. Fix what they report. 6. Write REPORT.md.  Budget: reproduce in 2 minutes, first test by 5, first edit by 10. If you still have not written code by then, you have misread the task — re-read this message. Do not browse. Do not read files you have not been told to read. Do not write a plan document. There is no human to review a plan.  Files: read and write ONLY inside your working directory. Never write to /tmp, /home, or anywhere outside the project — the permission system auto-rejects those calls, and in this pipeline a rejected tool call ends your run. If you need scratch space, use ./target/tmp/ inside the project.  When you are done, say DONE and stop.  ---  ## Contract you must obey (AGENTS.md)  # AGENTS.md — RBOPS (Redblue Autonomous Ops)  This file is the contract for every agent that RBOPS dispatches. It is injected verbatim into every phase context by `rbops/dispatch.sh`. It is law, not advice.  You are running inside an automated pipeline. There is no human in your loop. The only thing that makes your work count is that it passes a machine gate. Nothing you *say* about your work is evidence. Only what `rbops/verify.sh` proves is evidence.  ---  ## 0. What RBOPS is  RBOPS is a self-dispatching, self-auditing development pipeline for the **Redblue** programming language.  - **Dispatcher** (`rbops/dispatch.sh`) — picks the next eligible phase from   `rbops/phases.json`, runs it, records state, gates it, triggers the next tick. - **Manifest** (`rbops/phases.json`) — the single source of truth. Machine-readable.   If a phase is not in the manifest, it does not exist. - **Gate** (`rbops/verify.sh`) — the only authority on whether work is real. - **Auditor** (`.opencode/agent/auditor.md`) — periodically reads the codebase,   finds new defects and missing capabilities, and **writes new phases**. - **Bootstrap** — the endgame: Redblue compiles Redblue.  ### The state machine  ```   IDLE ──dispatch──▶ RUNNING ──verify ok──▶ REVIEW ──ok──▶ .done ──▶ next phase                       │                       ├─ verify fail ──▶ RETRY (attempt+1)                       ├─ model error ──▶ .deferred (retry later tick)                       ├─ max attempts ──▶ .blocked (needs human)                       └─ budget spent ──▶ .starved (needs new phases) ```  State is **git**, not a database. Each phase directory in `phases/` carries marker files that are committed. Stateless runners therefore work.  ---  ## 1. HARD RULES — violating any of these fails the phase  1. **Do not touch RBOPS itself.** Never edit `.github/workflows/`, `rbops/`,    `rbops/phases.json`, or any `.opencode/agent/*.md`. The bot token has no    `workflows` scope and a push touching `.github/` is refused. Do not waste a    phase discovering this. 2. **Never weaken a gate.** You may not modify `verify.sh`, delete a failing    test, add `#[ignore]`, add `.skip`, loosen an assertion, lower a threshold,    raise a timeout to dodge a hang, or comment out a test body. **Any of these    is an automatic phase failure and a review-blocking finding.** There is no    exception for "it was flaky". Flaky tests get fixed, not muted. 3. **No claiming success you did not achieve.** If `verify.sh` is red, the phase    is not done. Report the failure honestly in `REPORT.md` and exit. 4. **Do not run long builds locally.** CI is the only place `cargo build` runs.    Locally use `cargo check --all-targets` at most, and only if essential. 5. **Never commit secrets, API keys, tokens.** Check `.gitignore` covers    `.env`, `*.key`, `target/`. 6. **No destructive git.** No `push --force` to `main`, no `reset --hard` on    shared history, no rewriting other phases' commits. 7. **One phase, one concern.** If you discover work that does not belong to your    phase, do not do it. Record it in `phases/phase-NNN/FINDINGS.md` so the    auditor can promote it to a real phase. 8. **No unbounded speculation.** Do not rewrite the architecture, rename public    types, change the `.rb` file extension, or redesign the grammar without an    explicit phase that says so.  ---  ## 2. Redblue — what must never silently change  Redblue is a tree-walking interpreter: `Lexer → Parser → Analyzer → VM`. Its contract is *readable English-like source*.  **Language invariants.** A phase may not change these without explicitly re-opening the language design in its own PROMPT:  | Invariant | Why | |---|---| | `.rb` is the source extension | Tooling, `rb run`, LSP | | `to … end`, `if … end`, `for … end` use `end`, never braces | Core philosophy | | `set x to <expr>` is assignment | Grammar | | `say` prints | 100% of examples depend on it | | `Value` variants are `Nothing/Number/Text/YesNo/List/Record/Object/Function/Builtin` | Public API, `redblue::Value` is exported | | Errors are `Error::{Lexer,Parser,Analyzer,Runtime,Io}` | Public API | | Trailing-comma and `{interp}` string syntax | Parser tests | | Existing tests in `tests/` keep passing | — |  **Backwards compatibility.** `examples/*.rb` and `modules/*.rb` are the language's specification-by-example. Every phase must keep them working. The gate runs them.  ---  ## 3. Testing mandate — this is where most phases fail  > Every test must assert something that can fail.  Redblue's current test layer is broken in exactly this way, which is why Phase 1 exists. From the moment you touch behaviour, your code is held to the standard the rest of the project has not yet reached.  ### 3.1 Every test must have  1. **A real assertion.** A Redblue test must use `expect <expr> to be <value>`,    `expect <expr> to contain <value>`, or `assert.equal`. If your test only    "runs without crashing", it is a smoke test: label it `// smoke` and it will    not count toward the phase's test quota. 2. **At least one edge case.** The happy path alone is not a test. 3. **A named failure message.** `expect x to be 5` must be able to fail with a    useful message. 4. **Determinism.** No wall-clock, no filesystem outside a temp dir, no network,    no reliance on `HashMap` iteration order.  ### 3.2 Mandatory edge-case matrix  For **every** behaviour you implement or change, test at minimum:  - **Empty / zero / "nothing"** — empty list, empty text, `nothing`, `0`, `""` - **Singleton and boundary** — exactly one element; index `0` and index `len-1` - **Out of bounds** — index `-1`, index `len`, index `999` (must be a clean   runtime error, never a panic, never UB) - **Type mismatch** — number where text expected, record where list expected - **Type coercion boundaries** — `0/0`, `1/0`, `-0.0`, `NaN`, `±Infinity`,   `2^53±1` (precision), `-2^31`, integer overflow past `i64` - **Unicode and escapes** — empty text, `"`, `\`, newline in string,   emoji/CJK/RTL, combining marks, very long strings - **Nesting and recursion** — nested lists, nested records, 3+ closure/object   scopes, mutual recursion - **Duplicate and missing keys** — record with repeated keys, missing field access - **Malformed input** — unterminated string, unclosed `end`, stray token,   empty file, BOM, CRLF line endings, non-UTF8 bytes - **Resource and state** — file that does not exist, permission denied, path with   spaces, deeply nested call stack, infinite loop guard  Pick the rows that apply. A phase that changes the VM must justify in `REPORT.md` which rows are N/A and why.  ### 3.3 Test quota (hard floor, not a target)  The gate enforces a floor: **≥ 3 new `#[test]` functions or ≥ 2 new Redblue `test` blocks**, plus **≥ 1 test named `edge_*`**, **≥ 1 test that asserts a failure is produced**, and **zero** newly-skipped or newly-ignored tests.  Both mandatory tests must be expressible in whichever language you are working in. Write them like this:  | Requirement | Rust | Redblue | |---|---|---| | edge case test | `fn edge_empty_list() { … }` | `test "edge_empty list is rejected" do` | | asserts a failure | `assert!(r.is_err())` / `#[should_panic]` | `try … catch error … end` |  A Redblue test name is a **string literal**, so it is `test "edge_foo"`, with a quote after `test `. Likewise Redblue has no exceptions: `try … catch error` is its failure-catching construct, and a test that faults without one is not asserting anything. Both forms are recognised by the gate.  **Not every phase has a failure channel.** A linter reports problems as a diagnostics collection; a formatter and an LSP emit documents. There is no `Result::Err` to assert on, so the "asserts a failure is produced" rule cannot apply to them. Phases 015, 016 and 017 declare `failure_assert: "not_applicable"` and the gate **skips that one check and says so**. Every other phase keeps it at full strength — a phase that changes what the interpreter *executes* must prove it produces a clean error when it should.  Deliberately, the count floor is low. It exists to catch "no verification at all", not to judge sufficiency — a hard 6 once failed a genuinely good phase with 5 edge tests, which is the gate being smarter than the reviewer. Judging whether the tests COVER the change is the reviewer's job (AGENTS.md section 5: an untested branch is a MAJOR finding, fixed and re-gated). The gate is the floor — cheap, mechanical, never wrong in the pass direction. The reviewer is the ceiling.  ### 3.5 You must implement, not only test  Most rules in this file measure *verification*. None of them measure *ambition*, so the cheapest way to pass the gate would be to write tests for behaviour that already exists and change no implementation at all. That is not a phase.  Every phase in `rbops/phases.json` from 011 onward declares `must_touch` — the areas where that phase's work must land. The gate fails the phase if **no** changed file falls under any of them:  ```json "must_touch": ["src/"]                 // phases 011-013, 015, 016, 018, 019 "must_touch": ["src/", "modules/"]// phase 014, 024 — either is acceptable "must_touch": ["src/", "bootstrap/"]    // phases 021-023 "must_touch": ["tests/"]                // phase 020 — a harness, test-only by design ```  Read your own phase's entry before you start. If your finding genuinely cannot be fixed in the declared area, that is a manifest bug: say so in `FINDINGS.md` with the file:line, and fix it in the area the finding names rather than bending the phase. Phase-024 is the worked example — its finding cites both `src/lexer.rs` and `modules/MathUtils.rb`, so both are declared and either is a valid answer.  Note what this rule is **not**: it is a floor, satisfied by one changed line under `src/`. It says "you implemented something", not "you implemented enough". Judging that the change is correct, complete and proportionate is the reviewer's job, and an `edge_*` test that does not actually pin the new behaviour is a BLOCKER finding.  ### 3.4 The four gates, in order  ``` cargo fmt --check          # formatting        (fails on any diff) cargo clippy -- -D warnings# lints, zero warnings tolerated cargo test                 # full suite, 0 failures ./rbops/verify.sh          # project-specific gates ```  ---  ## 4. REPORT.md contract — required, machine-checked  `phases/phase-NNN/REPORT.md` must exist. `verify.sh` rejects the phase without it.  ```markdown # Phase NNN — <title>  ## What changed | File | Lines | What | |---|---|---| | src/vm.rs | +40 −12 | call depth counter in call_builtin |  ## Tests added | Test | Edge class covered | |---|---| | edge_call_stack_overflow | out of bounds / resource | | test_zero_division | type/numeric boundary |  ## Gates | Gate | Result | |---|---| | cargo fmt --check | pass | | cargo clippy -- -D warnings | pass | | cargo test | 42 passed, 0 failed | | rbops/verify.sh | pass |  ## Invariants touched - None  /  - `expect` now returns a catchable Runtime error (was: process abort)  ## Known gaps / follow-ups - No stack-depth limit for mutually recursive objects yet → FINDINGS.md ```  A REPORT.md with empty tables, or with a `Gates` table that does not match what `verify.sh` printed, is a **fake completion** and will be caught by the auditor.  ---  ## 5. Reviewer contract  The reviewer (`.opencode/agent/reviewer.md`) is **read-only**: no edit, no bash. It reviews the phase diff after the gate is green and emits `FINDINGS:`. Findings are then fixed by a fresh implementer run, and **the full gate is run again**. A phase with open BLOCKER/MAJOR findings is not `.done`.  The reviewer must specifically hunt for:  - **Fake completion** — REPORT.md claims a gate passed that it did not; tests   that cannot fail; assertions weakened; thresholds lowered. - **Panic paths** — any reachable `panic!`/`unwrap`/indexing/`unreachable!`. - **Unbounded resource use** — no recursion limit, no loop guard, no output cap. - **Nondeterminism** — `HashMap` iteration reaching output, floats in equality. - **Error-message regressions** — a previously-rejected program now accepted, or   a crash where an error was expected. - **Spec drift** — behaviour that now contradicts `SPEC.md` or `docs/GRAMMAR.md`.  ---  ## 6. Auditor contract — the phase-generating loop  Every N phases, the **auditor** runs. It must:  1. Measure the codebase: test count, coverage of `src/*.rs`, gate health,    `todo`/`unimplemented`/`FIXME` counts, largest functions, clippy debt. 2. Diff the language's actual behaviour against `SPEC.md` and `ROADMAP.md`    and list every gap as a concrete, file:line-anchored finding. 3. Turn each finding into a **new phase**: id, title, severity, dependencies,    estimated risk, and an acceptance gate — written into `rbops/phases.json`. 4. Refuse to invent work. A phase with no file:line evidence is not created.  The auditor is the only component permitted to append to `phases.json`.  ---  ## 7. Bootstrap contract — the endgame  **Goal:** Redblue compiles Redblue.  This is a ladder, not a leap. Each rung must be green before the next:  | Stage | Artifact | Definition of done | |---|---|---| | **S0** | Rust `rb` (exists today) | `cargo test` green, `rb` runs all `examples/*.rb` | | **S1** | `rb compile` — Redblue **bytecode compiler** written in Rust | emits `.rbc`; `rb vm file.rbc` runs it | | **S2** | The S1 compiler **rewritten in Redblue** | `rb run bootstrap/compiler.rb` produces byte-identical `.rbc` to S1 for a corpus | | **S3** | S2-compiled compiler compiles itself | fixed point: `stage1.rbc == stage2.rbc` byte-for-byte | | **S4** | S3 binary is the shipped `rb` | `rb` built by `rb` passes the full gate |  Non-negotiable rules for any phase in the S2–S4 range:  - **No shimming.** You may not add a Rust-only fast path that the Redblue path   silently falls back to. If it is not implemented in Redblue, it does not exist. - **Fixed-point is the only proof.** "It compiles" is not done. Byte-identical   output over a ≥200-program corpus is done. - **Self-hosting forbids changing the language to make it easier.** Any grammar   change needed for S2+ must be justified by general language merit in its own phase. - **The gate for every bootstrap phase is the S3 fixed-point test**, plus the   normal four gates. No exceptions, no "next phase will finish it".  ---  ## 8. Communication rules  - Report facts, not narrative. "`rbops/verify.sh` reports `edge_foo: FAILED`" beats "there may be an issue with foo". - When blocked, say exactly what you tried and what the exact error was. - Never fabricate a file:line. If you did not read the line, do not cite it. - Prefer the smallest correct change. A 400-line refactor in a bug-fix phase   is a review BLOCKER.  ---  # YOUR PHASE: phase-084  Project root: /home/runner/work/rbops/rbops/redblue  # phase-084 — Resolve the reviewer agent's permissions where opencode can actually see them  <!-- GENERATED by rbops/gen-prompts.sh from rbops/phases.json. DO NOT EDIT. -->  | | | |---|---| | Severity | `blocker` | | Risk | `medium` | | Depends on | _none_ | | Timeout | 90 min | | Must change | `tests/` |  ## You must change `tests/`  This phase declares `must_touch: [tests/]`. The gate fails the phase unless at least one changed file falls under it. Writing tests, documentation or a report is not enough — if you finish without touching that area, the phase fails on exactly that check.   ## Evidence — verified finding  ``` `~/.config/opencode/opencode.json` is 213 bytes and declares NO `agent` key - `jq -r 'has("agent")'` on it prints false. It sets only `default_agent: "build"` and a permission block whose `edit` and `bash` are both `allow`. The redblue checkout has no opencode.json (`ls redblue/opencode.json` -> No such file or directory). rbops/dispatch.sh:786 invokes opencode as `--dir "$PROJECT_DIR" --agent reviewer`; opencode resolves configuration from the working directory upward and then the global file, so the name `reviewer` is not found there and opencode falls back to `default_agent: build` - a configuration that grants edit and bash. The reviewer is contractually read-only: rbops/agents/reviewer.md opens 'You are READ-ONLY: you cannot edit files or run commands', and the pipeline's own opencode.json sets `agent.reviewer.permission` to `edit: deny, bash: deny`. NEITHER is in the resolution path. dispatch.sh:727-732 already acknowledges the resolution failure in prose and inlines the CONTRACT, which is why every phase has had one - but the PERMISSIONS remain unenforced, so the single component whose job is to catch a compromised phase is the one component that is allowed to edit it, and nothing in the gate would notice if it did. ```  **Re-verify this before you change anything.** If the finding no longer reproduces on `main`, stop and write that to `FINDINGS.md` — a stale phase must never be "fixed" by inventing a change.  ## Goal  Ship the agent definitions inside the checkout opencode resolves, so `--agent reviewer` and `--agent verifier` carry their declared permissions instead of inheriting the global build agent's.  ## Method  1. Read `AGENTS.md` in full. Obey every hard rule; they are not negotiable. 2. Reproduce the finding. Record the exact command and the exact wrong output. 3. Write the failing test FIRST. Watch it fail. Do not touch production code    until you have a red test that fails for the right reason. 4. Make the smallest change that turns it green. 5. Run the four gates yourself before claiming anything:     ```bash    cargo fmt --all -- --check    cargo clippy --all-targets -- -D warnings    cargo test --all-targets    ./rbops/verify.sh phase-084    ```  6. Write `REPORT.md` in the exact shape required by `AGENTS.md` section 4.  ## Definition of done  - [ ] the project ships an opencode.json whose `agent.reviewer.permission` denies BOTH edit and bash, and whose `agent.verifier.permission` allows both - [ ] a test reads that file and asserts all four permission values, so removing a single deny fails `cargo test` - [ ] a test asserts the file declares no `default_agent`, so the implementer agent cannot be quietly repointed - [ ] the file lives at the repository root and NOT under `.opencode/agent/`, which rbops/verify.sh:127 treats as a forbidden path - [ ] REPORT.md records the opencode configuration-resolution rule this relies on (working directory first, then the global file) with a citation, and says plainly that the test can only assert the file's contents, not opencode's resolution - [ ] edge_* tests cover: the file missing, the `agent` key missing, `reviewer` missing, `verifier` missing, and one permission flipped to allow - five distinct failing cases - [ ] `cargo test` reports 0 failures and `cargo clippy --all-targets -- -D warnings` is clean with no `allow(` added  ## Test requirements — non-negotiable  From `AGENTS.md` section 3. Tick each row that applies to this change. For every row you do NOT cover, write one line in `REPORT.md` saying why.  - [ ] empty — covered / N/A + why - [ ] singleton — covered / N/A + why - [ ] boundary — covered / N/A + why - [ ] out_of_bounds — covered / N/A + why - [ ] type_mismatch — covered / N/A + why - [ ] numeric_boundary — covered / N/A + why - [ ] unicode — covered / N/A + why - [ ] nesting_recursion — covered / N/A + why - [ ] duplicate_missing_keys — covered / N/A + why - [ ] malformed_input — covered / N/A + why - [ ] resource_limit — covered / N/A + why  You must also:  - at least 3 new `#[test]` functions (or at least 2 new Redblue `test` blocks).   This is a floor, not a target: it catches "no verification at all". Whether   your tests COVER the change is judged by the reviewer, and an untested branch   is a MAJOR finding that sends the phase back. - at least 1 test named `edge_*` - at least 1 test that asserts a **failure** is produced, not just a success - zero new `#[ignore]`, `// skip`, or `allow(clippy::` suppressions - zero newly-failing pre-existing tests  ## Constraints  - Do not touch `.github/workflows/`, `rbops/`, `.opencode/agent/`, or   `rbops/phases.json`. The gate rejects these paths, and the bot token has no   `workflows` scope so a push touching `.github/` is refused by GitHub. - Do not weaken, delete or re-scope any existing test. - Do not change the language surface listed in `phases/INVARIANTS.md`. - Keep the diff on-topic. A refactor smuggled into a bug-fix phase is a   review BLOCKER. - Do not update README or ROADMAP to claim this works. Update them only when   the gate is green and you have run the examples yourself.  ## If you are blocked  Do not fake progress. Write what you tried, the exact error, and what you believe is required into `FINDINGS.md`, then stop.  A blocked phase with an honest report is worth more than a green phase with a lie in it. `verify.sh` will reject the phase anyway; a fabrication only adds a blocking review finding on top.  --- ## RESUMED WORK — read this before touching anything A previous attempt's work is already merged into your tree. DO NOT start over and DO NOT rewrite it from scratch. Your job is to finish what is missing:  1. Run the four gates FIRST, before changing anything, to see the current state. 2. Read phases/phase-084/REPORT.md from the previous attempt (harvested below if    present) to see what it claimed and what the gate actually said. 3. Fix exactly what failed. Add what is missing. Do not remove working code.  ### Previous REPORT.md (do not trust its gate claims — verify them)  ``` # Phase 084 — Resolve the reviewer agent's permissions where opencode can actually see them  ## Reproduction (before any change)  ``` $ test -f opencode.json && echo EXISTS || echo MISSING MISSING $ ls -d .opencode ls: cannot access '.opencode': No such file or directory $ ls tests/ | grep -E 'opencode|agent' (no output) ```  The checkout ships no opencode config at any project scope. Per the opencode config documentation, project config is `./opencode.json`, `./opencode.jsonc` or `.opencode/opencode.json`, found by walking up from the working directory to the worktree root; global config is `~/.config/opencode/opencode.json`; the two are deep-merged with the project overriding the global. With no project file, `--agent reviewer` and `--agent verifier` find nothing in the project scope and inherit the global `default_agent: build`, whose `edit` and `bash` are `allow`. The finding reproduces.  The contract the reviewer is supposed to live under — `rbops/agents/reviewer.md` opening "You are READ-ONLY" — is prose passed in as a prompt, not a permission. Nothing enforced it. Reproduced, and it reproduces in the sense that matters: no file in the resolution path carries a permission for either name.  ## What changed  | File | Lines | What | |---|---|---| | `opencode.json` | +20 −0 | **New**, at the repository root. Declares `agent.reviewer` with `permission.edit = "deny"`, `permission.bash = "deny"`, and `agent.verifier` with `permission.edit = "allow"`, `permission.bash = "allow"`. No `default_agent`. | | `tests/agent_permissions_test.rs` | +359 −0 | **New**. 12 `#[test]` functions. Reads the shipped file and asserts all four permission values; 8 of them also drive the pure checker over deliberately broken documents. |  No file under `src/`, `rbops/`, `.github/workflows/`, `.opencode/agent/` or `rbops/phases.json` was touched. `cargo fmt --all` reformatted only the new test file — `git status` after formatting shows exactly two untracked files and no modified ones.  ### Why the file is at the root and not under `.opencode/agent/`  `opencode.json` is the project-config filename opencode looks for first at the working directory. `.opencode/agent/` is the directory for *agent definition files* (`<name>.md`), not for a config; a config there would not be in the resolution path for a top-level config read, and this project's gate treats `.opencode/agent/` as a forbidden path. `project_config_ships_at_the_repository_root` asserts the file's parent is the repository root, and that no competing copy exists under `.opencode/agent/` or `.opencode/`.  ### The configuration-resolution rule this relies on  opencode resolves configuration in two scopes, project first:  > | Scope | Path | > |---|---| > | Project config | `./opencode.json`, `./opencode.jsonc`, or `.opencode/opencode.json` (opencode walks up from the cwd to the worktree root) | > | Global config | `~/.config/opencode/opencode.json` or `~/.config/opencode/opencode.jsonc` | > > Configs from each scope are deep-merged. Project overrides global.  Citation: opencode's configuration documentation, "Where files live" (<https://opencode.ai/config.json> is the published JSON Schema the documentation points at; the same directory-of-configs table appears in the `customize-opencode` reference shipped with opencode). The schema at that URL was fetched and the shipped file was validated against it with `jsonschema.validate` — **SCHEMA VALID**, no error. `$schema`, `agent`, `description`, `mode` and `permission` are all in the published `Config`/`AgentConfig`/`PermissionConfig` definitions, and `"allow"`/`"deny"` are members of the `PermissionActionConfig` enum.  Because the merge is *deep*, the global file's permissive top-level `permission` block still contributes `edit`/`bash` keys underneath this file. Per-agent `permission:` overrides the top-level block, and it is declared here for **both** tools on **both** agents rather than relying on an override for one — which is why the tests assert all four values individually.  **What these tests cannot prove.** They read a file and assert its contents. They do not run `opencode`, so they cannot prove which file opencode opened, or that a given opencode build resolves the name `reviewer` to this definition. A future opencode that changed its resolution order would leave this suite green while the reviewer again ran with edit access. The test can prove the file says the right thing and fails the moment a `deny` is dropped, an agent is renamed, or a `default_agent` is reintroduced. Closing the remaining gap needs `rbops/dispatch.sh` to assert the resolved agent's permissions at startup — recorded in FINDINGS.md, not attempted here, since `rbops/` is off-limits.  ## Tests added  | Test | Edge class covered | |---|---| | `project_config_ships_at_the_repository_root` | file present, at root, no forbidden-path or duplicate copy | | `project_config_pins_reviewer_read_only_and_verifier_privileged` | asserts all four permission values individually | | `project_config_declares_no_default_agent` | `default_agent` absent — implementer agent cannot be repointed | | `edge_missing_config_file_is_rejected` | the reproduction itself: no file → failure | | `edge_missing_agent_key_is_rejected` | empty — `{}` and `{"$schema":"x"}`, both lack `agent` | | `edge_missing_reviewer_is_rejected` | duplicate/missing keys — only verifier declared | | `edge_missing_verifier_is_rejected` | duplicate/missing keys — only reviewer declared | | `edge_permission_flipped_to_allow_is_rejected` | the damaging edit — all four values flipped, each caught by name | | `edge_reviewer_without_permission_block_is_rejected` | nesting — `agent.reviewer` declared, `permission` absent | | `edge_permission_value_of_wrong_type_is_rejected` | type mismatch — boolean, number, nested object, null | | `edge_duplicate_permission_key_resolves_to_the_last_value` | duplicate keys — repeated `edit`, last value wins | | `edge_malformed_config_is_rejected` | malformed — empty file, truncated, trailing comma, BOM, non-object |  Eight of twelve assert a **failure** is produced (`expect_err` / `expect` on a `Result`). Five distinct failing documents are required by the phase and all five are present as separate tests: file missing, `agent` key missing, `reviewer` missing, `verifier` missing, one permission flipped to allow.  ### Mutation check — the claim, verified  A single-value change to the shipped file, reviewer `edit` `deny` → `allow`:  ``` $ cargo test --test agent_permissions_test test edge_permission_flipped_to_allow_is_rejected ... FAILED test project_config_pins_reviewer_read_only_and_verifier_privileged ... FAILED test project_config_ships_at_the_repository_root ... FAILED the error must name the flipped key, got: `agent.reviewer.permission.edit` is "allow", expected "deny" assertion `left == right` failed: `agent.reviewer.permission.edit` must be "deny" the shipped opencode.json must satisfy the agent permission contract: ".../opencode.json: `agent.reviewer.permission.edit` is \"allow\", expected \"deny\"" test result: FAILED. 9 passed; 3 failed ```  Three tests fail and every message names the exact key that is wrong. The file was restored immediately afterwards; the final suite state is green.  ## Gates  | Gate | Result | |---|---| | `cargo fmt --all -- --check` | pass — clean, no diff | | `cargo clippy --all-targets -- -D warnings` | pass — clean, no warnings, no `allow(` added | | `cargo test --all-targets` | **1569 passed, 0 failed, 0 ignored** (12 of them new in `agent_permissions_test`) | | `./rbops/verify.sh phase-084` | **NOT RUN — cannot run from this checkout** |  On the fourth gate: `./rbops/verify.sh` does not exist in this checkout. `test -x rbops/verify.sh` → absent; `ls -d rbops .opencode` → both absent. The pipeline that dispatched this phase lives outside the working directory and the phase brief instructs that it not be inspected. I am reporting this as not run rather than claiming a pass I did not observe. The gates it would enforce that I can check by hand are satisfied: no path under `.github/workflows/`, `rbops/`, `.opencode/agent/` or `rbops/phases.json` is in the diff; no test is skipped, ignored, deleted or weakened; no `allow(clippy::` was added.  ## Test-requirement matrix  | Row | Status | Where / why | |---|---|---| | empty | covered | `edge_missing_agent_key_is_rejected` — `{}`; `edge_malformed_config_is_rejected` — empty file | | singleton | covered | `edge_missing_reviewer_is_rejected` and `edge_missing_verifier_is_rejected` — exactly one agent declared | | boundary | covered | `edge_duplicate_permission_key_resolves_to_the_last_value` — the boundary between first and last occurrence of a repeated key, which is the boundary that decides whether the reviewer is writable | | out_of_bounds | N/A | nothing here is indexable or ranged. The nearest analogue — a tool key that is not one of the two declared — is covered by `edge_reviewer_without_permission_block_is_rejected` (absent key) and `edge_permission_value_of_wrong_type_is_rejected` (present but unusable). No index is ever computed, so there is no out-of-bounds to reach. | | type_mismatch | covered | `edge_permission_value_of_wrong_type_is_rejected` — boolean, number, nested object, and `null` where a string action belongs | | numeric_boundary | N/A | the config schema contains no numeric value. `edge_permission_value_of_wrong_type_is_rejected` passes `0` as a permission and proves it is rejected as a number rather than compared as a value | | unicode | N/A | no string is transformed by anything under test — `check_document` compares four literal action strings against a JSON document. There is no normalisation, no case folding, no encoding step that a unicode input could exercise. The one non-ASCII byte that matters is handled instead: the BOM case in `edge_malformed_config_is_rejected` | | nesting_recursion | covered | `edge_reviewer_without_permission_block_is_rejected` cuts the three-level `agent` → `reviewer` → `permission` chain at the last level; `edge_permission_value_of_wrong_type_is_rejected` cuts it at the fourth (`permission.edit`). No recursion: the walk is a fixed three-level descent | | duplicate_missing_keys | covered | `edge_duplicate_permission_key_resolves_to_the_last_value` (repeated key) plus the two missing-agent tests | | malformed_input | covered | `edge_malformed_config_is_rejected` — empty file, truncated JSON, trailing comma, BOM, top-level array | | resource_limit | N/A | the checker reads one file of a fixed size once, with no loop over unbounded input, no recursion, and no accumulation. The only bound worth pinning is the file's own size, and pinning it would be asserting the file is small rather than that the code is safe |  ## Invariants touched  None. This phase adds a configuration file and a test file. No change to the `.rb` extension, to `end`-delimited blocks, to `set x to <expr>`, to `say`, to the `Value` variants, to the `Error` enum, to trailing-comma or `{interp}` string syntax, and no change to anything under `src/`. `README.md` and `ROADMAP.md` are untouched.  ## Known gaps / follow-ups  - The suite proves the file's contents, not opencode's resolution. Closing that   requires `rbops/dispatch.sh` to resolve `--agent reviewer` and assert its   permissions are `deny`/`deny` before handing it the diff. → FINDINGS.md - `reviewer` is declared `mode: "primary"`, matching a CLI `--agent`   invocation. If dispatch ever invokes it as a subagent instead, the mode is   wrong. Nothing in this checkout can tell which it does; → FINDINGS.md - `reviewer` is granted neither `webfetch` nor `write`, so it falls back to the   global defaults for those. This phase pins only `edit` and `bash`, which is   what the finding names. Tightening the rest is a separate concern. →   FINDINGS.md ```
